of the  realm of possibility at this stage when  it comes to 2D Zelda I feel like we'll  be seeing even more remakes as opposed  to brand new titles an unfortunate Trend  in the current age of video games in my  opinion it's no surprise then that many  people have been predicting or  advocating for a similar concept as seen  with Mario a Zelda maker if you will  however other games even the top-down  installments are a very different Beast  compared to a Mario side scroller the  games are all about exploring a vast  World NPC interaction puzzles finding  hidden secrets and witnessing a story  unfold definitely a step up from running  and jumping from point A to point B as  many have pointed out one option would  be to narrow it down to only one aspect  of the series more specifically dungeons  which the series is famous for this  would indeed make it a lot more  manageable they are self-contained  challenges that can function without a  grander story but it also means that as  opposed to Mario Maker which can present  something very close to a genuine 2D  Mario experience a Zelda dungeon Builder  cannot after all dungeons are but one of  many elements that make a Zelda game a  Zelda game is still that doesn't mean  that a dungeon maker is a bad idea it  may not be able to substitute a real  Zelda experience but for a lot of people  dungeons are one of the most enjoyable  elements in the franchise to some extent  Nintendo already experimented with the  concept in the 2019 remake of Link's  Awakening though for reasons I'll get  into shortly it was a far cry from what  people in mind and Mark into a mini game  than an actual gameplay mode so how  would one go about creating something  like a dungeon maker what are the  limitations and roadblocks for a project  like this after looking into it for the  past weeks I can confidently say that  it's definitely not as straightforward  as Mario Maker because Zelda dungeons  deal with all kinds of mechanics that  can easily become problematic first and  foremost is finding a balance between  accessibility and versatility there's  plenty of examples of level editors map  makers and game design tools that are  either integrated into the game itself  or function as a mod or a standalone  title some are very basic and beginner  friendly While others are so complex  that they border on an actual gaming  engine which is definitely not for  everyone a big part of Mario Maker's  success is how intuitive it is the UI  manages to communicate most of the  available options purely through visuals  in the base tutorial in just a couple of  hours of fiddling around are enough to  get started there's no need for node  setups complicated game logic and  configurations or walls of text and  documentation simply by dragging and  dropping objects on top of each other  letting them enter act and messing  around with the different options and  you'll quickly get a grasp on what's  possible and once everything clicks the  only thing holding you back is your own  creativity I mean people have made fully  functional calculators with this thing  for a dungeon maker I think a similar  approach is absolutely necessary it  needs to be easy to understand yet  present enough options and versatility  to create some amazing stuff the last  thing you want is having to look up a  tutorial every two minutes but you also  don't want to play so many restrictions  that dungeons end up feeling way too  similar Link's awakening's dungeon  Creator definitely suffered from the  latter you cannot even create your own  custom Chambers instead you can only  choose from pre-existing ones and  arrange them into a grid these rooms are  also not unique to the Creator itself  and are simply recycled from The Game's  story dungeons furthermore all the  content inside the room are baked in the  amount of doorways whether it has locked  doors contains a chest the enemies and  so on as such it's impossible to create  your own puzzle scenarios Beyond how you  navigate the dungeon as a whole and even  this is severely limited as the game  logic often decides things for you you  depending on how the rooms are laid out  for example if you place a room with a  locked door and two accessible rooms to  the side of it each containing a chest  the key to the door will be in the first  chest you check or maybe it's randomized  I'm still not sure but the point is you  have no control over what's inside of a  chest same goes with staircases which  always seem to connect to each other  randomly I don't want to sound too harsh  or demanding because the dungeon Creator  in Link's Awakening is so obviously  meant to be an extra like I said before  it's more of a mini game and I actually  did have fun with it for a couple of  hours but if Nintendo ever decides to go  all the way and create a standalone  Zelda maker it's safe to say that it has  to be a lot meatier than this Nintendo  Lance wants something a bit Messier so  let's start with the basics creating an  overall dungeon layout and individual  rooms I don't think I have to explain  why 3D Zelda dungeons are out of the  question structures like these and their  Central mechanics are way too complex to  recreate in the type of level design  tool we're talking about they would  either have to be heavily simplified and  cut down in regards to what you can or  cannot do which goes against the whole  principle of a 3D Zelda dungeon or the  design tool itself would have to be at  the same level as something like Halo  forge which means losing accessibility  the 2D or top down format is a lot more  manageable in that regard the space is  clear and easy to understand there's  less directions and physics to deal with  and more importantly just like Mario  it's all grid based which I think is  essential to keep it comprehensible the  only consideration that remains is  whether you want to go for a true 2D  experience I.E Sprite bass Like A Link  to the Past or Minish Cap or a top-down  3D engine like Link's Awakening or Link  Between Worlds both options should work  fine as long as the level editor remains  top-down oriented and they could even go  with a combination between the two it's  not too difficult to create a 3D  environment that looks like a classic  Zelda dungeon from a bird's eye  perspective if you apply pixel based  textures as you can see here one major  benefit to this approach is that it  presents the option to switch between  many different art styles on the fly  from classic 8-bit to 16-bit to a more  realistic one as seen in links away  Awakening a similar option is already  present in Mario Maker which includes  three classic themes plus the new Super  Mario Bros art style which utilizes 3D  models whatever they choose to go with  the same basic rules apply regardless  and Nintendo already has the tools to do  both the Link's Awakening engine and as  you may remember they created a 2d demo  to test out some of breath of the Wild's  mechanics I do think that in the case of  Zelda there needs to be a lot more  texture and coloring options than we see  in Mario if not dungeons will quickly  end up looking the same or become  repetitive this can easily be fixed with  a wide variety of walls and Floors to  choose from ornaments and decorations  like Vines cobwebs debris and so on as  well as the option to change the Hue of  specific texture groups this way people  can express their creativity and make  their dungeons more unique I thought  about the option for custom texturing as  well but first of all the switch's  touchscreen is not exactly on the same  level as a drawing tablet and drawing  with an analog stick no thank you  secondly I'm not so sure if Nintendo  would put their faith in the maturity of  their demographic if you get my drift  when it comes to the editor there's  really only two types necessary to  construct a dungeon a level editor where  you'll be constructing individual rooms  and of course a map editor this is  obviously the starting point and it's  from here that you'll be creating  Chambers and arranging them into a  larger structure similar to Link's  Awakening but there's a couple of things  I would add to it first is multiple  floors which has become a staple of  Zelda dungeons it gives the structure  depth and verticality despite not being  full 3d not to mention you can only fit  so many rooms side by side in a grid  like this so additional floors will  allow you to build something a lot  bigger it also allows for more variation  in how you connect certain parts of the  dungeon as opposed to just walking from  room to room you can also Place chasms  elevators or stairways that take you to  a floor below second is the option to  create rooms of various sizes Link's  Awakening only lets you choose from  single screen dungeon rooms locations  that have a scrolling camera like the  central chamber of eagles tower for  example have all been excluded which is  quite limiting being able to create  spaces that span multiple grid blocks  would be a big Improvement in that  regard I do realize that there has to be  a limit to it if only for the sake of  the engine so let's say a maximum of  three or four blocks total I think this  would be enough to spice it up a bit  finally I would include the option to  save rooms as templates either from  within the map editor or inside the  level editor or both this way you can  create your own library of rooms which  can be transferred between different  dungeon projects and reused of course  not every room you create will be  something special so this should  definitely be optional as for the level  editor I think Mario Maker already laid  a solid groundwork placing objects in  terrain by drawing drag and drop copy  and erase group select the usual stuff I  was able to code a very basic room  editor in just a few days and I'm a  horrible programmer so it shouldn't be  much of a problem for the Wizards at  Nintendo granted mine is by no means an  accurate representation and cannot even  be played but you know it's a  demonstration nothing more I did run  into a bit of a conundrum whilst working  on it though I really like the idea of  being able to give the room a custom  shape and placing exits anywhere you  please it would definitely make the  dungeon more varied when you're not  restricted two rectangular rooms only  but it can pose a bit of a problem when  you're trying to connect them into the  larger structure you would have to keep  close track of exactly where you placed  your exits and make sure they're not  misaligned I don't really have a  definitive solution for it maybe the  placement of exits has to be restricted  to specific spots only like in the  middle and corners of the outer walls  for example or they could restrict  shaping the outer walls altogether and  instead let you choose from a  predetermined set of room shapes it is  more user-friendly this way but it does  take away from creative freedom and I  don't know if this is a good trade-off  maybe people don't mind correcting these  types of misalignments be sure to let me  know what you think so that covers the  basics for shaping the overall dungeon  structure there's definitely some  hurdles and roadblocks to consider and  overcome but I think overall it's more  than manageable but now we get to the  difficult stuff since what good is a  Zelda dungeon without enemies hazards  and of course puzzles now I think  enemies and Hazards don't pose much of a  problem either some pits here lava there  spike traps moving platforms all the  good stuff as for the enemies I'm sure  enough tendo has all the essential AI  for all the different types stored  somewhere as long as there's a decent  selection to choose from and they can  adapt to the layout of the room it  should work just fine the same goes for  bosses and mini bosses though for the  final boss they may want to consider the  same type of approach as Link's  Awakening and pair them up with a  pre-built boss room after all some of  them do require a certain setup to  accommodate their moveset like lava for  example but the real challenge lies in  creating puzzles which are an essential  part of a Zelda dungeon it's safe to say  that pre-made puzzle setups as seen in  Link's Awakening are definitely a no-go  since there's only so many scenarios  they can include sooner or later players  will have encountered every setup in the  game and thus they'll know how to solve  each and every one what you want to do  is give people the opportunity to come  up with their own puzzle scenarios from  scratch to start off you'll need a wide  selection of objects to work with like  different types of switches weapons  conveyor belts locks and keys moving  platforms just to name a few and enemies  can be utilized for this purpose as well  the next step is that these objects need  to be able to interact and talk to one  another in some way for certain objects  particularly physics-based ones this can  simply be pre-programmed by the  Developers for example moving a block  onto a floor switch will cause it to  activate or placing a movable object  onto a conveyor belt or a moving  platform will influence its momentum  this is nothing special and we've  already seen this kind of stuff in Mario  Maker these are rules set up by the  developers and don't require input from  the player but what about objects that  can trigger events through other memes  as an example if I create a room with a  closed door and a floor switch how will  the engine know that pressing this  switch should open up the door of course  you could pre-program door objects to  automatically detect if there's a switch  present in the room and react to it when  pressed but what if I don't want that  what if I want this floor switch to  spawn a chest instead and shooting this  eye switch will activate the door under  normal circumstances like in an actual  game engine you would need something  like nodes or straight up coding to give  instructions on what should happen to  Which object but preferably we want to  stay away from that kind of stuff so  let's take a look at an example from  another game namely time Splitters 2  which if you didn't play this game game  back in the day you were definitely  missing out because it was one of the  best console Shooters of its time in my  opinion made by some of the same people  who worked on Goldeneye 64. the amount  of content it provides is absolutely  stunning an amazing campaign awesome  music classic arcade lots and lots of  multiplayer modes and as a cherry on top  they included a fully fleshed out map  maker which for its time was so much  better than it had any right to be in  fact you can bet that in this day and  age a mode like this would be sold as 20  DLC or something they really don't make  games like these anymore you know what's  impressive about time Splitters map  maker is not the level creation itself  while it does have rooms of various  sizes they're all pre-made and the  choice is pretty limited it has the  option for multiple floors but there's  only a few different themes which don't  offer a whole lot of variety and the  choice of actors and objects is also  nothing to write home about but where  Chimes is in the option to insert your  own game logic into your level by  setting up triggers and actions you can  create your own events custom objectives  Mission structure briefings enemy  behavior and Patrol routes all without a  single line of code triggers can involve  anything from activating a switch  destroying an object reaching a certain  location on the map killing a certain  enemy having an enemy spot you and so on  these triggers can then be linked to a  specific action like making a key appear  spawning an enemy or weapon blowing up a  computer terminal and much much more by  linking all these different objects  together you can create all sorts of  scenarios mission objectives and chain  reactions for example I can destroy this  box which then kills this enemy which in  turn makes a key card appear so I can  open up this door it's pretty neat  there's essentially no limit to how many  actions can be linked to one single  trigger which allows for so many  different types of setups the only thing  I don't like about the logic section is  the UI it's all text based which makes  it hard to keep track of all the objects  and events you've set up and how they  relate to one another it's completely  disconnected from the map itself and it  took me a while to understand how the  system actually works the objects you've  applied logic to are visible on the map  screen and you can rotate and move them  around and such but you can see what  they do or what they are connected to  for a Zelda dungeon Builder you want to  have a similar system where you can link  objects to each other to trigger certain  actions but just like Mario Maker it's  preferable to do it visually right  inside the editor and not in a separate  disconnected screen filled with text the  types of objects you link together will  determine the action that follows  linking a door to a switch speaks for  itself the door will open once the  switch is pressed but linking a chest to  one or more enemies for example may turn  it invisible and will spawn as soon as  said enemies are defeated additionally  if something is linked to more than one  object say you connect a door to four  switches you could add even more options  like whether or not you have to press  all four or if any will do or you can  set up an order in which they need to be  pressed and then hide the Secret order  in the dungeon somewhere perhaps in the  level design with a system like this you  could practically recreate 90 of the  typical 2D Zelda puzzles and more I'm  sure there's plenty of flaws and  limitations to it stuff I haven't even  thought about yet but it's the best  solution I can currently think of and if  they include enough gizmos and  combination to work with you can create  some pretty nifty puzzle scenarios of  course there's a lot more to think about  and consider when it comes to a Zelda  dungeon but I think we have the basics  covered consider this as a sort of pitch  to Nintendo I guess I'm not actually  planning to make a dungeon Builder  myself nor do I claim to be able to I'd  love to take on the challenge someday  but right now I've got other things to  do man the most important aspect is that  it remains as user-friendly as possible  while at the same time providing enough  options to create something unique and  exciting despite its overall Simplicity  people have created some truly amazing  stuff in Mario Maker by exploiting the  options available the last thing to  mention is that the system should be  able to catch and autocorrect as much as  possible what I mean by that is that the  game should be able to account for  oversights without immediately making  the dungeon itself unplayable every time  you want to test the dungeon you want to  avoid a long list of error messages  because certain things aren't properly  set up which is something that was kind  of annoying in Link's Awakening in  TimeSplitters 2 for example if a door or  hallway doesn't connect to anything it  simply turns into a wall automatically  if you place only one warp Point without  a destination it just sits there and  doesn't do anything the same should  apply to a dungeon maker disconnect the  doors turning into walls staircases that  don't lead anywhere should either do  nothing or be invisible when the level  compiles and if you place a traversable  Chasm but forget to put a room  underneath to land on it'll  automatically turn into a bottomless pit  at the same time you also don't want to  take away too much freedom I thought  about issues like having more locked  doors than there are Keys potentially  soft locking those who play your level  which I'm sure there's a solution for  but at the same time I think people  should also be left to their own devices  players will no doubt create dungeons  that are extremely unfair but just like  Mario maker that's what the voting  system is for if your dungeon sucks  others will surely let you know the only  restriction I do think is fair is that  just like in Mario Maker you have to be  able to beat your own level before being  allowed to upload it so even if it's  totally unfair you'll at least know that  there is a way to get to the end and  that is all I have to say about it maybe  one day we'll see something like this  being realized or maybe it's a lot more  difficult than I make it out to be I'm  not even close to working out every  aspect of it so I'm sure there's a  developer out there who's like yeah not  gonna happen that said there are some  amazing Indie projects in development  right now that showcase the potential of  stuff like Dynamic Landscaping like this  project by oh boy Anastasia opara who  was kind enough to let me use some of  their footage there's a link to their  social media accounts in the description  below so you can follow their progress  for yourself while this may not apply to  a top-down Zelda game very well in terms  of perspective it does illustrate how  terrain walls and Floors can  intelligently adjust and reshape  themselves to fit their surroundings  which just looks so cool in my opinion  and as a coding method it may be useful  for a future Zelda maker as well anyway  a massive thanks to everyone who  supports the channel to narrow for  letting me use some of his footage a big  fan of your content by the way to local  hourglass for joining my patreon and  Arya pie Rodrigo Lunas and hyrule's  Warrior for becoming a member thank you  all very much it means a lot I will see  you all next time this is Dawn signing  off and have a good one  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]

